1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
Moving on to meningitis, this is inflammation of the leptomeninges.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:07,740
In order to understand this, let's go back and remind ourselves that here is the skull,

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:12,320
and then tightly attached to the skull is a layer of the meninges called the dura,

4
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:18,060
and then here is the brain, and then tightly attached to the brain is a layer of the meninges called the pia.

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:24,280
And then in between, you've got these web-like spaces, which then result in a layer called the arachnoid.

6
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:28,540
And so these are the three layers of the meninges, the pia, the arachnoid, and the dura.

7
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:32,820
And some people like to say that there is a pad in between the brain and the skull.

8
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:36,060
Now, the dura is called the dura because it's thick and durable.

9
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,540
And the arachnoid and the pia tend to be very light and thin,

10
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,740
and so they're called the leptomeninges or the light meninges.

11
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:48,560
And with that in mind, then you can understand that meningitis is inflammation of the leptomeninges,

12
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,620
i.e. the pia and the arachnoid.

13
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,920
Here's a picture of meningitis, and you can clearly see the brain.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,140
And so when you see the brain, you're looking at the pia.

15
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:01,120
The dura would be durable and thick, and you would have difficulty seeing the surface of the brain clearly.

16
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:07,540
And so, again, within the pia, you can see here that you've got all this inflammation and exudate and pus,

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,660
and so this represents actually a bacterial meningitis.

18
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,320
Meningitis is most commonly due to an infectious agent.

19
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,700
It's very high yield to know the agents and the age groups in which they occur.

20
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:23,060
So, for example, in a neonate, the three most common causes are group B strep, E. coli, and listeria.

21
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,580
A group B strep and E. coli can arise from the vaginal canal during the birthing process.

22
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:33,420
In fact, in the case of group B strep, we'll test women in the third trimester to see whether they're colonized with group B strep,

23
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:40,080
and if they are, we actually treat them with antibiotics to decrease the risk of exposure of the baby during the birthing process.

24
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,060
Neisseria meningitis is the most common cause in children and teenagers.

25
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:48,720
Board examiners like you to know that this enters through the nasopharynx and then goes to the blood,

26
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:51,560
and from the blood, it gets to the meninges.

27
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,260
Strep pneumo is the most common cause in adults and the elderly.

28
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:59,080
H flu would be a common cause in non-vaccinated infants.

29
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:03,960
Board examiners like to go after that, especially with all the controversy over immunizations.

30
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:08,020
Coxsackievirus is the most common viral cause of meningitis.

31
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:14,360
It has a fecal-oral transmission, and then fungi can cause meningitis in immunocompromised individuals.

32
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:18,800
Patients with meningitis present with a classic triad of headache, neck stiffness, and fever.

33
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,580
The headache and neck stiffness are due to the fact that the meninges are inflamed,

34
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:26,200
and of course, the meninges wrap around the brain, giving headache and neck stiffness

35
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:31,020
because when you bend the neck, you're going to feel the pain of the meninges being inflamed.

36
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:33,520
Fever, of course, because the patient has an infection.

37
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
In addition, you might see photophobia, especially with virus.

38
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,820
It's much more common to see photophobia.

39
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:42,700
Vomiting can be present, and altered mental status can also be seen.

40
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,720
When you suspect meningitis, you'll do a lumbar puncture.

41
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:50,360
The goal is to sample the fluid in the subarachnoid space and then to analyze it.

42
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:54,980
Board examiners like you to know that you should normally stick the needle between L4 to L5,

43
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:56,460
the level of the iliac crest.

44
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,180
The reason being, which is particularly high yield, is that the spinal cord ends at L2,

45
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:05,000
but the subarachnoid space and the cauda equina, they continue to S2.

46
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:11,100
And so, again, if you stick a needle at L4 to L5, you'll be able to get the subarachnoid space without hitting the cord.

47
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:15,540
Board examiners also like you to know that when you do an LP, you're actually going to go through a few layers.

48
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,140
You go through the skin, and then you cross the ligaments.

49
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:20,120
Then you'll enter the epidural space.

50
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:25,080
You'll cross the dura, and then you'll cross the arachnoid, but you will not pierce the pia,

51
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:26,760
and that's particularly high yield.

52
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,700
Again, you will not pierce the pia.

53
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:32,720
Once you obtain your fluid, you can then analyze the fluid to determine the cause.

54
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:36,740
So, for example, neutrophils with low glucose would be seen with bacteria.

55
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:40,860
Now, neutrophils, of course, because that's the cell that fights bacterial infection,

56
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:45,260
and then in addition, the glucose would be low because the bacteria, they consume glucose.

57
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:51,320
Now, recall that the normal glucose in the CSF is about two-thirds of that what is present in the serum.

58
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:56,700
So, for example, if the serum glucose is normally 100, then the normal CSF glucose would be about 66.

59
00:03:57,380 --> 00:04:01,840
And so, bacteria, they'll consume glucose, giving you a low glucose, so neutrophils plus low glucose.

60
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:05,800
In addition, you'll often see a positive gram stain and culture.

61
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:10,580
In the case of viral meningitis, you'll see lymphocytes, and that's because lymphocytes fight virus.

62
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:15,000
And then you'll see a normal CSF glucose, and that's because virus does not consume glucose.

63
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,860
I mean, if you want to talk about virus, the discussion is are they living or are they dead,

64
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:21,300
so forget about the question about eating glucose.

65
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,980
They do not consume glucose, and so the glucose will be normal.

66
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:28,440
And finally, in the case of fungal meningitis, you'll also see lymphocytes.

67
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:34,340
However, because fungi consume glucose, there'll be a low CSF glucose as opposed to the viral meningitis.

68
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:41,000
One last high yield point, and that is that the complications of meningitis are classically seen with bacterial meningitis.

69
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:45,380
Again, the idea here is that there really is no space in the head except for the brain.

70
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:47,160
It's a very tight space.

71
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:52,000
And if you get bacterial meningitis, you get a ton of pus and inflammation and inflammatory exudate,

72
00:04:52,260 --> 00:04:56,100
and that then creates pressure on the brain, which can then cause the brain to herniate.

73
00:04:56,440 --> 00:04:59,000
And when you get a herniation, that's what then results in death.

74
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:05,940
And of course, this would only be seen with bacteria because it's with bacteria that you get the massive amounts of exudate and pus, etc.

75
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:11,340
Now, another series of complications with bacterial meningitis are all based on the healing process.

76
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:17,840
After you get bacterial meningitis, you're going to have massive tissue damage, which will then result in extensive healing and fibrosis.

77
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:23,300
And that fibrosis can knock out the normal pathways of the CSF, giving you hydrocephalus.

78
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:28,140
It can damage the nerves as they exit the brain, so for example, giving you hearing loss.

79
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:31,380
And it can scar the surface of the brain, increasing the risk for seizures.

80
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:36,620
But the take-home point here is that all of this would be primarily seen with bacterial meningitis.

81
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:38,760
And that concludes our section on meningitis.

